# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the TradingApp test suite.
#
# The Qt libraries are NOT TSan-instrumented (that would require a TSan build
# of Qt itself), so TSan cannot see Qt-internal synchronization — e.g. the
# lock-free atomics coordinating QNetworkAccessManager's HTTP worker thread —
# and reports races inside Qt that are not analyzable here. These
# suppressions silence reports whose intercepted call originates in the Qt
# libraries; races in the project's own code remain fully visible.
called_from_lib:libQt6Network.so
called_from_lib:libQt6Core.so
called_from_lib:libQt6Test.so

# Issue #20 (2026-08-13, tst_etoroclient TS_CLI_007_marketOpenFollowsQuoteAdvance,
# one occurrence right after a multi-hour Axivion run under elevated load; not
# reproducible in 3 isolated re-runs nor any other sanitize pass): TSan reported
# JsonHttp::handleReply's finished()-lambda connection being torn down by
# QObjectPrivate::ConnectionData::deleteOrphaned on a worker thread while the main
# thread concurrently constructed a connection on the same QNetworkReply. Traced
# JsonHttp.cpp/EtoroClient.cpp end to end: neither this project's code, nor the
# test's MockHttpServer, ever creates a QThread or calls moveToThread — every
# JsonHttp/EtoroClient/QNetworkReply instance here lives and dies on the main
# thread. The other side of the race is QNetworkAccessManager wiring the SAME
# QNetworkReply to its own internal HTTP delegate thread at request-issue time
# (the "HTTP worker thread" the comment above already names). The
# called_from_lib rules above did not catch this specific report because much of
# QObjectPrivate::ConnectionData's bookkeeping — including deleteOrphaned — is
# defined inline in Qt's private headers, so it is compiled INTO this project's
# own translation units (here, JsonHttp.cpp) rather than into libQt6*.so; the
# stack frame TSan attributes it to is therefore ours, not Qt's shared library.
# This is the same category of unanalyzable Qt-internal race the file already
# documents, just one whose frame resolves to project code due to header
# inlining rather than to a .so. JsonHttp::handleReply also now uses
# Qt::SingleShotConnection (defence in depth: narrows our own connection's
# lifetime), but that cannot reach into QNAM's own internal wiring, hence this
# suppression for the specific Qt-internal function named in the report.
race:*ConnectionData*deleteOrphaned*
